/* PaKuWord – English daily pack 2026-11-25: new domain Cooking (30) + 30 entries in existing domains */
window.PW_CONTENT = window.PW_CONTENT || {};
window.PW_CONTENT.en = (window.PW_CONTENT.en || []).concat([
/* ---------- COOKING ---------- */
{id:"cook_mise_en_place",cat:"cook",t:"Mise en place",
 s:"Getting all ingredients and tools ready and in their place before you start cooking.",
 l:"Mise en place means preparing everything before the heat goes on: washing and chopping vegetables, measuring spices, opening tins and setting out pans and spoons. Professional cooks arrange their station like this so that during a busy service they never have to stop and search. At home it makes cooking calmer and safer, because nothing burns while you hunt for the garlic. It is less about tidiness for its own sake and more about thinking the recipe through first.",
 x:"She read the recipe twice and did her mise en place, so the stir-fry took only ten minutes.",
 f:"The phrase is French and means, word for word, putting in place; chefs often use it for any careful preparation, not only in the kitchen.",
 js:"Getting everything ready before you start to cook.",
 jl:"Before cooking, good cooks put out all the things they need: the food, already washed and measured, and the bowls, spoons and pans. Then they don't have to run around looking for things while something is bubbling on the hob.",
 jx:"Before baking cookies with Dad, Leo put the flour, butter, sugar and bowl on the table."},
{id:"cook_umami",cat:"cook",t:"Umami",
 s:"A savoury, deep, mouth-filling taste, often called the fifth basic taste.",
 l:"Besides sweet, sour, salty and bitter, our tongues can sense umami, a rich savoury taste. It comes mainly from glutamate and certain related substances found in foods such as parmesan, tomatoes, mushrooms, soy sauce, meat broth and seaweed. Ripening, drying, ageing and fermenting often increase umami, which is why aged cheese and ripe tomatoes taste so full. Umami is not the same as saltiness: it makes food taste satisfying and rounded rather than simply salty.",
 x:"A spoonful of miso gave the vegetable soup a lovely umami depth.",
 f:"The Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda identified glutamate as the source of this taste in nineteen hundred and eight, after studying a broth made from kombu seaweed.",
 js:"A rich, savoury taste, like in cheese, tomatoes or soup.",
 jl:"Your tongue can taste sweet, sour, salty and bitter. Scientists found a fifth taste too, called umami. It is the yummy, savoury taste in things like tomato sauce, cheese, mushrooms and chicken soup.",
 jx:"Grandma's tomato sauce tastes so good because it is full of umami."},
{id:"cook_maillard",cat:"cook",t:"Maillard reaction",
 s:"The browning reaction between proteins and sugars that gives toast, roasts and fried food their flavour.",
 l:"When food containing proteins and sugars is heated well above the boiling point of water, they react and form hundreds of new flavour and aroma compounds and a brown colour. This is the Maillard reaction, and it is behind the crust of bread, the taste of toast, roasted coffee and a browned steak. It needs fairly dry, hot surfaces, which is why food crowded in a wet pan steams instead of browning. It is different from caramelisation, which involves sugar alone.",
 x:"Pat the mushrooms dry before frying, or the Maillard reaction will not have a chance to brown them.",
 f:"It is named after the French chemist Louis-Camille Maillard, who described the reaction in nineteen twelve.",
 js:"The change that makes toast and roast potatoes golden brown and tasty.",
 jl:"When bread turns into toast, it goes brown and smells wonderful. That happens because tiny bits in the food join together in the heat and make new tastes and smells. Scientists call it the Maillard reaction.",
 jx:"The golden crust on our pizza is the Maillard reaction at work."},
{id:"cook_caramelisation",cat:"cook",t:"Caramelisation",
 s:"The browning of sugar when it is heated, creating a nutty, sweet and slightly bitter flavour.",
 l:"When sugar is heated strongly, at roughly one hundred and sixty degrees Celsius and above, it melts, turns golden and then brown, and develops complex flavours. This is caramelisation, used for caramel sauce, crème brûlée tops and toffee. Onions are often said to caramelise when cooked slowly, though there the Maillard reaction also plays a part because onions contain proteins too. Caramel can turn from perfect to burnt in moments, so it needs constant attention, and hot sugar causes serious burns.",
 x:"He cooked the onions slowly for forty minutes until they were caramelised and sweet.",
 f:"The word caramel came into English from French, which took it from the Spanish caramelo.",
 js:"When sugar gets very hot and turns into golden, sticky caramel.",
 jl:"If sugar is heated a lot, it melts and turns golden brown. That is caramel. It is very, very hot, so only grown-ups should make it, but it tastes great on desserts.",
 jx:"Mum made caramel for the pancakes while I watched from a safe distance."},
{id:"cook_emulsion",cat:"cook",t:"Emulsion",
 s:"A mixture of two liquids that normally do not mix, such as oil and water, with tiny droplets of one spread through the other.",
 l:"Oil and water separate on their own, but if you whisk them hard, one breaks into tiny droplets inside the other. To keep it from separating again, cooks use an emulsifier, a substance that holds the droplets apart. In mayonnaise this is lecithin from egg yolk; in vinaigrette mustard helps for a while. When an emulsion fails and separates, cooks say it has split. Many sauces, dressings and even ice cream depend on emulsions.",
 x:"Add the oil drop by drop, or the emulsion will split and the mayonnaise will be runny.",
 f:"Milk is a natural emulsion: tiny drops of fat floating in a watery liquid.",
 js:"A mix of oil and water that stays mixed, like mayonnaise.",
 jl:"Usually oil floats on top of water. But if you shake or whisk them very hard with something like egg yolk or mustard, they stay together as a smooth mix. That mix is called an emulsion.",
 jx:"We shook oil, lemon juice and mustard in a jar and made a salad dressing emulsion."},
{id:"cook_roux",cat:"cook",t:"Roux",
 s:"A mixture of flour and fat cooked together and used to thicken sauces and soups.",
 l:"To make a roux, you melt butter or another fat, stir in an equal amount of flour and cook it for a few minutes. Then you slowly whisk in liquid such as milk or stock, and the flour thickens it without forming lumps. A white roux, cooked briefly, is the base of béchamel sauce; a longer-cooked, darker roux tastes nuttier but thickens less. Cooking the flour also removes its raw, floury taste.",
 x:"Start the cheese sauce with a roux of butter and flour, then add the milk bit by bit.",
 f:"In Louisiana cooking, gumbo is often made with a very dark roux, cooked slowly until it is the colour of chocolate.",
 js:"Butter and flour cooked together to make sauces thick.",
 jl:"To make a thick, smooth sauce, cooks melt butter, stir in flour and cook it a little. Then they slowly pour in milk while stirring. The sauce gets thick, like the one on macaroni cheese.",
 jx:"I stirred the roux while Mum poured in the milk for the lasagne sauce."},
{id:"cook_blanching",cat:"cook",t:"Blanching",
 s:"Boiling food very briefly and then cooling it quickly, usually in iced water.",
 l:"To blanch vegetables, you drop them into boiling water for a short time, often one to three minutes, and then plunge them into very cold water to stop the cooking. This keeps green vegetables bright, makes them slightly tender and helps peel tomatoes or peaches easily. Before freezing vegetables, blanching slows down natural enzymes that would otherwise spoil their colour, taste and texture in the freezer. It is a quick partial cooking, not a full one.",
 x:"Blanch the green beans so they stay bright for the salad.",
 f:"The word comes from the French blanchir, to whiten, because blanching was once used to whiten foods such as almonds.",
 js:"Cooking food very quickly in boiling water and then cooling it in cold water.",
 jl:"Sometimes cooks put vegetables in boiling water for just a minute or two and then straight into icy water. The vegetables stay bright green and a little crunchy. A grown-up should handle the boiling water.",
 jx:"Dad blanched the broccoli, and it stayed as green as a crayon."},
{id:"cook_braising",cat:"cook",t:"Braising",
 s:"Cooking food slowly in a covered pot with a small amount of liquid, usually after browning it first.",
 l:"Braising usually starts by browning meat or vegetables in a hot pan, then adding a little liquid such as stock, wine or water, covering the pot and cooking gently for a long time on the hob or in the oven. The slow, moist heat breaks down tough connective tissue, so cheaper, harder cuts of meat become soft and tender. Unlike stewing, braising often uses larger pieces and less liquid. Many dishes around the world, from pot roast to goulash, use this method.",
 x:"The beef was braised for three hours until it fell apart with a fork.",
 f:"The word comes from the French braise, meaning glowing embers, because pots were once set in the embers with hot coals placed on the lid.",
 js:"Cooking food slowly in a covered pot with a little liquid.",
 jl:"Braising is a slow way of cooking. The food sits in a pot with a lid and a little bit of liquid, and it cooks gently for a long time. At the end it is very soft and full of flavour.",
 jx:"The braised carrots were so soft that my little sister could squash them with a spoon."},
{id:"cook_simmer",cat:"cook",t:"Simmer",
 s:"To cook liquid gently just below boiling, with small bubbles rising slowly.",
 l:"When a liquid simmers, small bubbles rise and break gently at the surface, rather than the big rolling bubbles of a full boil. Simmering is used for soups, stocks, sauces and stews, because it cooks food evenly and keeps it from breaking apart or sticking. A hard boil can make stock cloudy and meat tough. Recipes often say to bring something to the boil and then turn the heat down to a simmer.",
 x:"Let the soup simmer for twenty minutes with the lid half on.",
 f:"High in the mountains water boils at a lower temperature, so food simmering there cooks more slowly.",
 js:"To cook something gently with only small, slow bubbles.",
 jl:"When soup is simmering, you see little bubbles popping slowly, not big jumping ones. Gentle cooking like this keeps the soup from splashing and burning, and the vegetables cook nicely.",
 jx:"The pot of soup simmered quietly while we laid the table."},
{id:"cook_al_dente",cat:"cook",t:"Al dente",
 s:"Cooked so that pasta or vegetables are tender but still slightly firm when bitten.",
 l:"Al dente describes pasta, rice or vegetables that are cooked through but keep a little resistance in the middle. Italian cooks prefer pasta this way because it has a pleasant bite and holds sauce better, while overcooked pasta turns soft and sticky. The usual test is simply to take out a piece and bite it a minute or two before the time on the packet. Pasta often finishes cooking for a moment in the sauce, so taking it out al dente helps it not go mushy.",
 x:"Drain the spaghetti while it is still al dente and toss it straight into the sauce.",
 f:"In Italian the phrase means to the tooth, referring to the gentle bite you feel.",
 js:"Pasta that is cooked but still a little bit firm.",
 jl:"Al dente is Italian and means to the tooth. It's how many people like their pasta: soft enough to eat, but not floppy or mushy. You can check by tasting one piece.",
 jx:"I tasted one piece of pasta and said, it's al dente, it's ready!"},
{id:"cook_deglazing",cat:"cook",t:"Deglazing",
 s:"Adding liquid to a hot pan to loosen the browned bits stuck to the bottom and turn them into a sauce.",
 l:"After you fry meat or vegetables, a layer of browned, sticky bits often stays on the pan. These bits are packed with flavour from the Maillard reaction. To deglaze, you pour in a liquid such as stock, wine, water or juice while the pan is hot and scrape the bottom with a wooden spoon, so the bits dissolve. The result is the start of a tasty pan sauce. It also makes the pan easier to wash.",
 x:"After frying the chicken, she deglazed the pan with a little stock to make the gravy.",
 f:"Chefs call the browned layer on the pan the fond, a French word meaning base or bottom.",
 js:"Pouring a little liquid into a hot pan to get the tasty brown bits off.",
 jl:"After frying, there are brown, stuck-on bits in the pan. They are full of flavour! Cooks pour in some liquid and scrape them up with a spoon. This turns them into a yummy sauce.",
 jx:"Dad deglazed the pan, and the gravy tasted even better than last time."},
{id:"cook_reduction",cat:"cook",t:"Reduction",
 s:"Boiling or simmering a liquid so that some water evaporates, making it thicker and stronger in flavour.",
 l:"Reducing a sauce means cooking it uncovered so that water turns into steam and escapes, leaving a smaller amount of thicker, more concentrated liquid. Cooks reduce stocks, wine, fruit juices and vinegars to make glossy sauces and glazes. Because salt does not evaporate, a sauce that tasted right at the start can become too salty after reducing, so it is wise to season at the end. A wide pan reduces faster than a narrow one because more surface is exposed.",
 x:"The balsamic vinegar reduction was sweet and syrupy, perfect on the strawberries.",
 f:"Some classic French sauces are made by reducing stock to a small fraction of its original amount, until it becomes thick and almost jelly-like when cool.",
 js:"Cooking a sauce for longer so some of the water goes away and it gets thicker.",
 jl:"When a sauce cooks without a lid, some of its water turns into steam and floats away. What's left is thicker and tastier. Cooks call this a reduction.",
 jx:"We let the berry sauce reduce until it was thick enough to drizzle on ice cream."},
{id:"cook_stock",cat:"cook",t:"Stock",
 s:"A flavoured liquid made by simmering bones, meat or vegetables in water, used as a base for soups and sauces.",
 l:"Stock is made by simmering bones, meat scraps, vegetables, herbs and spices in water for a long time, then straining it. It gives flavour and body to soups, risottos, gravies and stews. Stock made with bones contains gelatine, which can make it set like jelly when cold. Vegetable stock is quicker and suits vegetarian dishes. People often use the words stock and broth interchangeably, though broth sometimes means a lighter liquid meant to be eaten on its own.",
 x:"She keeps the vegetable peelings in the freezer to make stock on Sundays.",
 f:"Stock cubes, a quick dried form of stock, became popular in the early twentieth century.",
 js:"A tasty liquid made by cooking vegetables or bones in water for a long time.",
 jl:"Cooks put vegetables, herbs and sometimes bones in a big pot of water and cook it slowly. Then they pour it through a sieve. The tasty liquid is called stock, and it makes soups and sauces delicious.",
 jx:"Our soup is made with homemade vegetable stock."},
{id:"cook_marinade",cat:"cook",t:"Marinade",
 s:"A seasoned liquid in which food is soaked before cooking, to add flavour.",
 l:"A marinade usually combines something acidic, such as lemon juice, vinegar or yoghurt, with oil, herbs, spices, garlic or soy sauce. Meat, fish, tofu or vegetables are left in it for anything from a few minutes to a day. Most of the flavour stays near the surface, and strong acids left too long can make fish or meat mushy. For safety, food should marinate in the fridge, and a marinade that held raw meat must be boiled before being used as a sauce.",
 x:"The chicken spent the night in a yoghurt and spice marinade before going on the grill.",
 f:"The word comes from the Spanish marinar, to pickle in brine, which is linked to mar, the sea.",
 js:"A tasty sauce that food soaks in before it is cooked.",
 jl:"Before cooking, people sometimes leave food in a mix of juice, oil and spices. It sits in the fridge and soaks up the flavour. That mix is called a marinade.",
 jx:"We put the tofu in a honey and soy marinade before baking it."},
{id:"cook_fermentation",cat:"cook",t:"Fermentation",
 s:"A process in which tiny living things such as bacteria or yeasts change food, often making it sour, fizzy or longer-lasting.",
 l:"In fermentation, microorganisms feed on sugars in food and turn them into acids, gases or alcohol. Bacteria turn milk into yoghurt and cabbage into sauerkraut or kimchi, while yeasts make bread rise and turn grape juice into wine. For thousands of years fermentation helped people keep food from spoiling before fridges existed. It also creates sour, tangy flavours. Home fermenting needs clean equipment and the right amount of salt, because the wrong microbes can grow if conditions are poor.",
 x:"Sauerkraut is made by fermentation: just cabbage, salt and time.",
 f:"In the eighteen fifties and sixties Louis Pasteur showed that fermentation is caused by living microorganisms.",
 js:"When tiny living things change food, like milk into yoghurt.",
 jl:"There are tiny living things, too small to see, called bacteria and yeasts. Some of them are helpful! They change food: they turn milk into yoghurt and help bread dough puff up. This change is called fermentation.",
 jx:"Our class watched bread dough grow because of fermentation."},
{id:"cook_gluten",cat:"cook",t:"Gluten",
 s:"A group of proteins in wheat, barley and rye that makes dough stretchy.",
 l:"When flour made from wheat, barley or rye is mixed with water and kneaded, its proteins join into a stretchy network called gluten. This network traps gas, which is why bread can rise and have a chewy texture. Bakers use strong, high-protein flour for bread and softer flour for cakes, where less gluten keeps them tender. Some people must avoid gluten for health reasons, for example people with coeliac disease, so food labels list it clearly.",
 x:"Kneading the dough develops the gluten and makes the bread springy.",
 f:"The word gluten comes from Latin and means glue.",
 js:"Something in flour that makes dough stretchy.",
 jl:"When you mix flour with water and squish it for a long time, the dough becomes stretchy, like elastic. That stretchiness comes from gluten. Some people's bodies can't take gluten, so they eat special gluten-free food.",
 jx:"When I pulled the pizza dough, it stretched because of the gluten."},
{id:"cook_proving",cat:"cook",t:"Proving",
 s:"Leaving yeast dough to rest so that it rises before baking.",
 l:"After kneading, yeast dough is left in a warm place, usually covered, so the yeast can feed on sugars and produce carbon dioxide gas. The gas gets trapped in the gluten network and the dough rises. Many breads are proved twice: once in the bowl and again after shaping. If dough is under-proved, the bread is dense; if over-proved, it may collapse in the oven. A simple test is to press the dough gently: it should spring back slowly.",
 x:"The rolls need to prove for another half an hour before they go in the oven.",
 f:"The word comes from the idea that the rise proves the yeast is alive and working.",
 js:"Letting bread dough rest so it puffs up.",
 jl:"Bread dough with yeast needs a rest in a warm place. While it rests, the yeast makes tiny bubbles of gas, and the dough slowly gets bigger. This waiting time is called proving.",
 jx:"We covered the dough with a tea towel and waited for it to prove."},
{id:"cook_raising_agent",cat:"cook",t:"Bicarbonate of soda and baking powder",
 s:"Two raising agents that release gas bubbles to make cakes and biscuits light.",
 l:"Bicarbonate of soda, also called baking soda, is sodium bicarbonate. It releases carbon dioxide when it meets an acid, such as yoghurt, buttermilk, lemon juice or cocoa. Baking powder already contains bicarbonate of soda together with a dry acid, so it only needs liquid and heat to work. That is why recipes with acidic ingredients often use soda and others use powder. Too much of either can give a soapy or bitter taste.",
 x:"The muffins were flat because I forgot the baking powder.",
 f:"Ready-made baking powder sold in small packets became popular at the end of the nineteenth century, thanks partly to the German pharmacist August Oetker.",
 js:"White powders that make cakes puff up and become fluffy.",
 jl:"Cakes are light because of tiny bubbles inside. Baking powder and baking soda help make those bubbles when they get wet and warm. Without them, many cakes would be flat like pancakes.",
 jx:"I measured one teaspoon of baking powder for our banana bread."},
{id:"cook_bain_marie",cat:"cook",t:"Bain-marie",
 s:"A bowl or dish set over or in hot water so that food heats gently and evenly.",
 l:"A bain-marie, or water bath, protects delicate food from direct heat. For melting chocolate, a heatproof bowl is placed over a pan of barely simmering water, without touching the water. For baked custards and cheesecakes, the dish sits in a tray of hot water in the oven. Water cannot get hotter than its boiling point, so the food cooks slowly and evenly and is less likely to burn, curdle or crack.",
 x:"Melt the chocolate in a bain-marie so it does not burn.",
 f:"The name means Mary's bath in French and is often linked to Maria the Jewess, an early alchemist, though the story is old and uncertain.",
 js:"A bowl of food put over warm water so it heats up gently.",
 jl:"Chocolate burns easily in a pan. So cooks put it in a bowl over a pot of hot water. The steam warms the bowl slowly, and the chocolate melts smooth and shiny.",
 jx:"We melted chocolate in a bain-marie to dip the strawberries."},
{id:"cook_tempering",cat:"cook",t:"Tempering chocolate",
 s:"Melting, cooling and gently rewarming chocolate so that it sets glossy and snaps cleanly.",
 l:"Cocoa butter in chocolate can set into several different crystal structures. If melted chocolate simply cools, it often turns dull, soft and streaky. Tempering means carefully controlling the temperature, heating, cooling and slightly rewarming the chocolate, so that only the stable crystals form. Tempered chocolate is shiny, snaps when broken and does not melt instantly in your fingers. Chocolatiers use it for bars, bonbons and decorations.",
 x:"The chocolate decorations were dull because she had not tempered the chocolate.",
 f:"Cocoa butter can form six different crystal forms, and only one of them gives the glossy finish and crisp snap people expect.",
 js:"Melting and cooling chocolate in a special way so it becomes shiny.",
 jl:"Chocolate makers warm chocolate, cool it down and warm it a little again, very carefully. This makes the chocolate shiny and snappy when you break it. It is called tempering.",
 jx:"The chocolate bar went snap, because it was tempered properly."},
{id:"cook_julienne",cat:"cook",t:"Julienne",
 s:"Cutting vegetables or other food into long, thin strips like matchsticks.",
 l:"To julienne a carrot, you cut it into thin slices, stack them and then cut them into fine strips of even size. Even pieces cook at the same speed and look neat in stir-fries, salads and garnishes. Other classic cuts include brunoise, tiny cubes made from julienne strips, and chiffonade, thin ribbons of leafy herbs. Learning to julienne safely means keeping fingertips curled under, away from the blade.",
 x:"Julienne the peppers and carrots for the spring rolls.",
 f:"A tool called a mandoline can cut julienne strips quickly, but it is so sharp that cooks often wear cut-resistant gloves.",
 js:"Cutting food into thin sticks, like matchsticks.",
 jl:"Julienne is a fancy word for cutting vegetables into long, thin sticks. It looks pretty in salads. Knives are sharp, so children should only try it with a grown-up and a safe knife.",
 jx:"The carrot sticks in my lunchbox were cut julienne style."},
{id:"cook_smoke_point",cat:"cook",t:"Smoke point",
 s:"The temperature at which a cooking oil or fat starts to smoke.",
 l:"Every oil or fat begins to smoke when heated past a certain temperature, called its smoke point. Beyond it, the fat breaks down, tastes bitter and gives off irritating smoke, and if heated further it can catch fire. Refined oils usually have higher smoke points than unrefined ones, and butter smokes sooner than most oils because of its milk solids. A pan of oil that catches fire must never be put out with water; turn off the heat and cover it with a lid or a fire blanket.",
 x:"She chose an oil with a high smoke point for frying, and saved the unrefined one for the salad.",
 f:"Clarified butter, with the milk solids removed, can be heated much hotter than ordinary butter before it smokes.",
 js:"The temperature when cooking oil gets so hot it starts to smoke.",
 jl:"If oil in a pan gets too hot, it starts to smoke. That means it is too hot and can be dangerous. Only grown-ups should cook with hot oil. If oil ever catches fire, never pour water on it – a grown-up covers the pan with a lid.",
 jx:"The oil started to smoke, so Dad turned the heat down straight away."},
{id:"cook_cross_contamination",cat:"cook",t:"Cross-contamination",
 s:"The spread of harmful germs from one food, surface or utensil to another.",
 l:"Raw meat, poultry, fish and eggs can carry bacteria that cause food poisoning. If the same knife, chopping board or hands touch raw chicken and then a salad, the germs can move across to food that will not be cooked. To prevent this, cooks keep raw and ready-to-eat food apart, use separate boards, wash hands with soap and clean surfaces. Raw meat is best stored on the bottom shelf of the fridge so its juices cannot drip onto other food.",
 x:"To avoid cross-contamination, use one board for raw meat and another for vegetables.",
 f:"Many professional kitchens use colour-coded chopping boards, for example red for raw meat and green for salad and fruit.",
 js:"When germs from raw food get onto other food.",
 jl:"Raw meat and raw eggs can have germs on them. If you touch raw meat and then a sandwich without washing your hands, the germs can go onto the sandwich. That is why we wash our hands and use different boards.",
 jx:"After touching the raw eggs, Zara washed her hands before cutting the fruit."},
{id:"cook_sous_vide",cat:"cook",t:"Sous vide",
 s:"Cooking food sealed in a bag in a water bath kept at a precise, steady temperature.",
 l:"In sous vide cooking, food is sealed in an airtight bag, usually with the air removed, and placed in water heated by a device that keeps the temperature exact. Because the water is never hotter than the target temperature, meat or fish cooks evenly from edge to centre and is very hard to overcook. The food usually gets a quick sear in a hot pan afterwards for colour and flavour. Correct temperatures and times matter for food safety.",
 x:"The restaurant cooks its salmon sous vide and then crisps the skin in a pan.",
 f:"The term is French for under vacuum, and the method was developed for restaurant cooking in France in the nineteen seventies.",
 js:"Cooking food in a sealed bag in warm water.",
 jl:"Some cooks put food in a closed plastic bag and put the bag in warm water. A machine keeps the water at exactly the same warmth for a long time. The food cooks slowly and evenly. It is called sous vide.",
 jx:"Uncle Tom cooked eggs sous vide, and they were perfectly soft."},
{id:"cook_seasoning",cat:"cook",t:"Seasoning (season to taste)",
 s:"Adding salt, pepper, herbs, spices or acid to bring out and balance the flavour of food.",
 l:"Seasoning means adjusting the taste of a dish, most often with salt and pepper, but also with herbs, spices, lemon juice or vinegar. Recipes that say season to taste ask you to taste and decide yourself, because ingredients and preferences vary. Small amounts of salt make food taste more like itself, and a squeeze of acid can brighten a dull dish. It is easier to add more than to take away, so cooks season little by little and taste as they go.",
 x:"Taste the soup at the end and season it with a pinch of salt and some lemon.",
 f:"Salt does more than add saltiness: it also reduces how strongly we taste bitterness, which is why a pinch can improve some vegetables.",
 js:"Adding a little salt, pepper or herbs to make food taste better.",
 jl:"Seasoning is when you add things like salt, pepper or herbs to food. Good cooks add a little, taste, and then decide if they need more. A little bit makes food taste just right.",
 jx:"Mum let me taste the soup and decide if it needed more seasoning."},
{id:"cook_folding",cat:"cook",t:"Folding",
 s:"Gently mixing a light, airy ingredient into a heavier one so the air stays in.",
 l:"Folding is used when whipped egg whites or whipped cream must be combined with a heavier mixture, as in mousse, soufflé or sponge cake. Instead of stirring or beating, you cut down through the middle with a spatula or large spoon, sweep along the bottom and lift the mixture over the top, turning the bowl as you go. This combines the two while keeping as many air bubbles as possible, which is what makes the result light.",
 x:"Fold the egg whites into the chocolate slowly, or the mousse will be flat.",
 f:"Egg whites whip up best in a very clean bowl, because even a little fat or yolk stops them from holding air; copper bowls have long been used to make them extra stable.",
 js:"Mixing very gently so the bubbles stay in.",
 jl:"Fluffy whipped egg whites are full of air bubbles. If you stir them hard, the bubbles pop. So cooks mix very gently, lifting and turning with a big spoon. That is called folding.",
 jx:"I folded the whipped cream into the strawberries slowly and carefully."},
{id:"cook_en_sunday_roast",cat:"cook",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Sunday roast",
 s:"A traditional British main meal of roasted meat, potatoes and vegetables, eaten on Sundays.",
 l:"A Sunday roast, also called Sunday lunch or a roast dinner, usually brings together roasted beef, chicken, lamb or pork with roast potatoes, vegetables and gravy, often eaten in the early afternoon. Each meat has its traditional partners, such as Yorkshire puddings with beef, mint sauce with lamb and apple sauce with pork. Vegetarian roasts, with a nut roast or a pie, are now common too. For many families it is the week's main shared meal, and many pubs serve one.",
 x:"We go to Grandma's every week for a Sunday roast.",
 f:"The French have long used the nickname les rosbifs, the roast beefs, for the English.",
 js:"A big family dinner of roast meat or a veggie roast, potatoes and vegetables on Sunday.",
 jl:"In Britain, many families have a special meal on Sundays. There are roast potatoes, vegetables, gravy and roast meat, or a vegetarian roast. Everyone sits down together, and there is usually a lot of food!",
 jx:"My favourite part of the Sunday roast is the crispy potatoes."},
{id:"cook_en_yorkshire_pudding",cat:"cook",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Yorkshire pudding",
 s:"A puffy, crisp savoury pudding made from batter of eggs, flour and milk, baked in hot fat.",
 l:"A Yorkshire pudding is not a dessert: it is a savoury batter of eggs, flour and milk, poured into very hot fat in a tin and baked until it puffs up, crisp outside and soft inside. It is traditionally served with roast beef and gravy. In parts of Yorkshire it was once eaten as a separate first course, so that a filling portion of pudding would make the more expensive meat go further. The batter rises because steam forms inside it in the hot oven.",
 x:"Don't open the oven door too early, or the Yorkshire puddings will sink.",
 f:"The name Yorkshire pudding appeared in Hannah Glasse's popular cookbook The Art of Cookery, published in seventeen forty-seven.",
 js:"A puffy, crispy batter cup eaten with roast dinner.",
 jl:"A Yorkshire pudding is made from eggs, flour and milk. It goes into a very hot oven and puffs up into a crispy cup. People eat it with roast dinner and lots of gravy. Even though it's called a pudding, it isn't sweet.",
 jx:"I filled my Yorkshire pudding right to the top with gravy."},
{id:"cook_en_full_english",cat:"cook",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Full English breakfast",
 s:"A large cooked breakfast with eggs, bacon, sausages, beans, tomatoes, mushrooms and toast.",
 l:"A full English breakfast is a big, hot plate usually including fried or scrambled eggs, bacon, sausages, baked beans, grilled tomato, mushrooms and toast, sometimes with black pudding or hash browns. Few people eat it every day; it is more of a weekend, holiday or café treat. Versions exist across the British Isles, such as the full Scottish, the full Welsh and the Ulster fry, each with local additions. Vegetarian and vegan versions are widely available.",
 x:"The hotel served a full English breakfast, so we did not need lunch.",
 f:"It is often called a fry-up, because many of its parts are traditionally fried in the same pan.",
 js:"A big cooked breakfast with eggs, beans, toast and more.",
 jl:"A full English breakfast is a huge, warm breakfast. There can be eggs, baked beans, toast, tomatoes, mushrooms and sausages. Most people only have it on special days, like holidays or weekends.",
 jx:"On holiday, Dad ordered a full English breakfast and couldn't finish it."},
{id:"cook_en_afternoon_tea",cat:"cook",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Afternoon tea",
 s:"A light meal of tea with sandwiches, scones and cakes, served in the afternoon.",
 l:"Afternoon tea is a small, elegant meal usually served on a tiered stand: finger sandwiches with the crusts cut off, scones with clotted cream and jam, and small cakes, all with pots of tea. Today it is mostly a treat for birthdays and celebrations, often in hotels and tearooms. It should not be confused with high tea, which historically meant a more substantial evening meal eaten by working families, or with tea used as a word for the evening meal in parts of Britain.",
 x:"For Mum's birthday we booked afternoon tea with tiny sandwiches and scones.",
 f:"The custom is usually credited to Anna, Duchess of Bedford, who in the eighteen forties began taking tea and snacks to bridge the long gap before a late dinner.",
 js:"A fancy snack time with tea, little sandwiches, scones and cakes.",
 jl:"Afternoon tea is a special treat. You get tiny sandwiches, scones with jam and cream, and little cakes, often on a plate stand with several floors. Grown-ups drink tea, and children might have juice or hot chocolate.",
 jx:"At afternoon tea I ate three scones and a tiny pink cake."},
/* ---------- MEDICINE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"m_virus_bacteria",cat:"med",t:"Virus and bacterium",
 s:"Two different kinds of germs: bacteria are tiny living cells, viruses are even smaller particles that can only multiply inside cells.",
 l:"Bacteria are single-celled organisms that can live and multiply on their own; most are harmless or useful, such as those in our gut, but some cause infections like strep throat. Viruses are much smaller and cannot reproduce without entering a living cell and using it to make copies; colds, flu and chickenpox are caused by viruses. This difference matters, because antibiotics work against bacteria but not against viruses, so they do not help with a cold.",
 x:"The doctor explained that the infection was viral, so antibiotics would not help.",
 f:"Most viruses are far too small to see with an ordinary light microscope; scientists first saw them clearly only with the electron microscope in the twentieth century.",
 js:"Two kinds of tiny germs; medicines called antibiotics only work on bacteria.",
 jl:"Germs are too small to see. Bacteria are tiny living things, and many of them are actually good for us. Viruses are even tinier, and they need to get inside our body's cells to make more of themselves. A cold is caused by a virus.",
 jx:"Our teacher said a cold is caused by a virus, so we should rest and drink water."},
{id:"m_dehydration",cat:"med",t:"Dehydration",
 s:"A state in which the body has lost more water than it has taken in.",
 l:"Dehydration happens when we lose more fluid than we drink, for example through sweating in heat or exercise, fever, vomiting or diarrhoea. Early signs include thirst, a dry mouth, tiredness, headache, dark yellow urine and passing urine less often. Babies, young children and older people become dehydrated more easily. Mild dehydration usually improves with drinking fluids; oral rehydration solutions replace lost salts too. Severe dehydration, with confusion, fainting or no urine, is an emergency.",
 x:"After the long hike in the sun, he had a headache from dehydration.",
 f:"Oral rehydration solution, a simple mix of water, salts and sugar, has saved millions of lives, especially of children with diarrhoea.",
 js:"When your body doesn't have enough water.",
 jl:"Your body needs water to work well. On hot days, when you run around or when you are ill, you lose water, for example by sweating. If you don't drink enough, you can feel tired, get a headache or feel very thirsty. That is dehydration.",
 jx:"After football, I drank a big bottle of water so I wouldn't get dehydrated."},
{id:"m_concussion",cat:"med",t:"Concussion",
 s:"A mild brain injury caused by a blow or jolt to the head that temporarily affects how the brain works.",
 l:"Concussion can follow a knock to the head or a sudden shake of the body, for example in a fall, a road accident or contact sport. A person does not have to lose consciousness to have it. Symptoms can include headache, dizziness, confusion, memory problems, nausea, sensitivity to light and feeling not quite right. Most people recover with rest and a gradual return to activities, but anyone with concussion should stop playing sport and get checked. Repeated vomiting, worsening headache or drowsiness need urgent help.",
 x:"After the collision on the pitch, the player was taken off for a concussion check.",
 f:"Many sports now follow the rule if in doubt, sit them out, meaning a player with a possible concussion should not return to the game that day.",
 js:"A head injury from a bump that can make you dizzy or confused for a while.",
 jl:"If you bang your head hard, your brain can get shaken inside your skull. You might feel dizzy, sick or have a headache. Always tell a grown-up if you hit your head, and stop playing for that day.",
 jx:"When Ollie bumped his head at the playground, the teacher checked him for concussion."},
{id:"m_ecg",cat:"med",t:"ECG (electrocardiogram)",
 s:"A quick, painless test that records the electrical activity of the heart.",
 l:"An electrocardiogram, or ECG, records the tiny electrical signals that make the heart beat. Small sticky pads called electrodes are placed on the chest, arms and legs and connected to a machine, which draws the signals as a wavy line. The test takes a few minutes and does not hurt. Doctors use it to check heart rhythm and rate and to look for signs of problems such as a heart attack. It records electricity; it does not send electricity into the body.",
 x:"In the emergency department they did an ECG because of his chest pain.",
 f:"The Dutch doctor Willem Einthoven developed a practical electrocardiograph at the start of the twentieth century and won the Nobel Prize in nineteen twenty-four for it.",
 js:"A test that draws a picture of how your heart is beating.",
 jl:"Your heart works with tiny electric signals. In an ECG test, a nurse puts small stickers on your chest, arms and legs. A machine reads the signals and draws a zigzag line. It doesn't hurt at all.",
 jx:"Grandpa had an ECG, and he said the stickers just felt a bit cold."},
{id:"m_pulse",cat:"med",t:"Pulse (heart rate)",
 s:"The rhythmic beat felt in an artery each time the heart pumps blood; usually counted per minute.",
 l:"Each heartbeat pushes blood into the arteries, creating a wave you can feel as a pulse, for example on the inside of the wrist below the thumb or at the side of the neck. Counting the beats for a minute gives the heart rate. It rises during exercise, fever, excitement or fear and falls during rest and sleep. Doctors also check whether the pulse is regular. Fit athletes often have a slower resting pulse, because a strong heart pumps more blood with each beat.",
 x:"The nurse counted his pulse while looking at her watch.",
 f:"Use two fingers, not your thumb, to take someone's pulse, because the thumb has a pulse of its own that can confuse the count.",
 js:"The beat you can feel in your wrist or neck when your heart pumps.",
 jl:"Put two fingers gently on the inside of your wrist, below your thumb. Can you feel a little tap, tap, tap? That's your pulse! It comes from your heart pumping blood. After running, it goes faster.",
 jx:"In PE we checked our pulse before and after running."},
{id:"m_en_aande",cat:"med",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"A&E (Accident and Emergency)",
 s:"The hospital department in the UK for serious injuries and illnesses that need urgent treatment.",
 l:"A&E, short for Accident and Emergency and also called the emergency department, treats people with serious and life-threatening problems, such as chest pain, breathing difficulty, severe bleeding, serious injuries or signs of a stroke. Patients are assessed by triage and seen in order of urgency, not arrival, so waits can be long for less serious problems. For urgent but not life-threatening problems, people are encouraged to use NHS 111, a GP, a pharmacy or an urgent treatment centre instead.",
 x:"He broke his arm skateboarding and spent the evening in A&E.",
 f:"In the UK the emergency phone number is nine nine nine; the European number one one two also works.",
 js:"The part of the hospital that helps people who are badly hurt or very ill straight away.",
 jl:"A&E is a part of the hospital that is open day and night. People go there when something is very serious, like a badly hurt arm or trouble breathing. Doctors see the most poorly people first.",
 jx:"When Sam fell off the climbing frame and hurt his arm badly, his mum took him to A&E."},
/* ---------- LAW (+6) ---------- */
{id:"l_constitution",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"Constitution",
 s:"The basic rules that set out how a country is governed and how power is limited.",
 l:"A constitution sets out who makes laws, who governs and judges, and what rights people have. The United Kingdom is unusual because it has no single written constitution document. Instead, its constitution is made up of Acts of Parliament, court decisions, long-standing conventions and important historical documents. A central principle is parliamentary sovereignty, meaning Parliament can make or change any law. Because of this, major constitutional changes can be made by ordinary Acts of Parliament.",
 x:"In our politics class we compared the unwritten British constitution with the American one.",
 f:"Magna Carta, agreed in twelve fifteen, is often seen as an early step towards limiting the power of the ruler, and a few of its clauses are still part of English law.",
 js:"The most important rules about how a country is run.",
 jl:"A constitution is like the main rule book of a country. It explains who makes the laws and what rights people have. In the UK, these rules are not all in one book but in many laws and traditions.",
 jx:"Our class made a constitution for the school council with rules everyone agreed on."},
{id:"l_tenancy",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Tenancy agreement",
 s:"A contract between a landlord and a tenant that sets out the rules for renting a home.",
 l:"A tenancy agreement explains how much rent is paid and when, how long the tenancy lasts, who is responsible for repairs and how either side can end it. In England and Wales, landlords who take a deposit for most private tenancies must protect it in a government-approved deposit protection scheme. Landlords must keep the property safe, for example with yearly gas safety checks, while tenants must pay rent and look after the home. Agreements cannot remove rights that the law gives tenants.",
 x:"Read the tenancy agreement carefully before you sign it.",
 f:"For most tenancies in England, the deposit is capped at five weeks' rent under the Tenant Fees Act of twenty nineteen.",
 js:"An agreement about renting a home.",
 jl:"Some families pay money each month to live in a home that belongs to someone else, called a landlord. They sign an agreement with rules: how much to pay, and who fixes things that break. That's a tenancy agreement.",
 jx:"When we moved into our flat, Mum and the landlord signed the tenancy agreement."},
{id:"l_alibi",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Alibi",
 s:"Evidence that a person was somewhere else when a crime happened, so they could not have done it.",
 l:"An alibi is a defence based on location: the accused says they were elsewhere at the time of the offence. It may be supported by witnesses, receipts, phone records or camera footage. In criminal cases in England and Wales, a defendant who plans to rely on an alibi must give details of it in advance, so the police can check it. In everyday speech, alibi is also used loosely for any excuse, but in law it means being in another place.",
 x:"His alibi was confirmed by the restaurant's camera footage.",
 f:"Alibi is a Latin word meaning elsewhere.",
 js:"Proof that someone was in a different place when something happened.",
 jl:"In detective stories, someone may say: it couldn't be me, I was at the cinema! If people saw them there, that's their alibi. It shows they were somewhere else.",
 jx:"Tom's alibi was strong: he was at football practice when the cake disappeared."},
{id:"l_verdict",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Verdict and sentence",
 s:"The verdict is the decision whether someone is guilty; the sentence is the punishment given after a guilty verdict.",
 l:"In a criminal trial in the Crown Court, the jury decides the verdict, guilty or not guilty. If the defendant is found guilty or pleads guilty, the judge then decides the sentence, such as a fine, community order or prison term, following sentencing guidelines. In magistrates' courts the magistrates decide both. People often mix up the two words, but they are separate stages. A not guilty verdict ends the case; the person is acquitted.",
 x:"The jury returned a verdict of not guilty, and he was free to go.",
 f:"Verdict comes from medieval Latin veredictum, meaning truly said.",
 js:"The verdict is the decision in court; the sentence is what happens next.",
 jl:"At the end of a trial, someone decides whether the person did the wrong thing or not. That decision is the verdict. If they did, the judge decides what should happen to them. That is the sentence.",
 jx:"In our class mock trial, the jury gave a verdict of not guilty."},
{id:"l_trademark",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"Trade mark",
 s:"A sign such as a name, logo or slogan that distinguishes one business's goods or services from others'.",
 l:"A trade mark helps customers recognise who a product comes from. In the UK, trade marks can be registered with the Intellectual Property Office, which gives the owner the right to stop others using the same or a confusingly similar mark for similar goods. Registration lasts ten years and can be renewed again and again. The symbol with a small capital R in a circle may only be used for registered marks, while the TM symbol can be used to claim any mark.",
 x:"The bakery registered its name as a trade mark before opening more shops.",
 f:"The first trade mark registered in the UK, in eighteen seventy-six, was the red triangle of the Bass brewery.",
 js:"A special name or logo that shows which company made something.",
 jl:"Lots of companies have a logo or a special name. A trade mark means other companies are not allowed to copy it. Then people know which company really made the thing they buy.",
 jx:"Our class invented a juice brand and drew our own trade mark."},
{id:"l_en_habeas_corpus",cat:"law",only:1,c:"England & Wales",t:"Habeas corpus",
 s:"An old legal right that lets a person who is detained have a court check whether the detention is lawful.",
 l:"Habeas corpus is a court order requiring that a detained person be brought before a judge, so the court can decide whether they are being held lawfully. If not, they must be released. It protects against imprisonment without a lawful reason, and it has been called one of the foundations of liberty in English law. The right was strengthened by the Habeas Corpus Act of sixteen seventy-nine and spread to many legal systems based on English law, including the United States.",
 x:"Her lawyer applied for habeas corpus, arguing she had been held without lawful authority.",
 f:"The Latin words mean that you have the body, from the opening words of the old court order.",
 js:"A rule that nobody can be locked up without a court checking it is fair.",
 jl:"A long time ago in England, people made an important rule: if someone is locked up, a judge must check whether that is allowed. If there is no good legal reason, the person must be set free. That rule has a Latin name, habeas corpus.",
 jx:"Our history teacher said habeas corpus protects people from being locked up unfairly."},
/* ---------- EVERYDAY LANGUAGE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"g_antonym",cat:"gen",t:"Antonym",
 s:"A word that means the opposite of another word.",
 l:"Antonyms are pairs such as hot and cold, open and closed, or buy and sell. Some are gradable, with many steps in between, like warm and cool; others are absolute, like alive and dead, where there is no middle. Some opposites look at the same action from two sides, like teacher and student. Antonyms help writers make contrasts, and many English antonyms are formed with prefixes, such as happy and unhappy or possible and impossible.",
 x:"The antonym of generous is mean.",
 f:"Some words can be their own antonyms: to dust can mean to remove dust, as in cleaning, or to add it, as when dusting a cake with sugar.",
 js:"A word that means the opposite, like big and small.",
 jl:"Antonyms are opposite words: up and down, happy and sad, fast and slow. You can play a game where one person says a word and the other quickly says its opposite.",
 jx:"The antonym of noisy is quiet – like our classroom after the bell!"},
{id:"g_homophone",cat:"gen",t:"Homophone",
 s:"A word that sounds the same as another but has a different meaning and often a different spelling.",
 l:"Homophones are words like there, their and they're, or flower and flour, which sound alike but mean different things. In speech, context usually tells us which one is meant; in writing, spelling shows the difference, and mixing them up is a very common mistake. Homophones are a favourite source of jokes and puns. For people who listen to texts read aloud, homophones can be confusing, so clear context matters even more.",
 x:"Knight and night are homophones: they sound the same but mean very different things.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek homos, same, and phone, sound.",
 js:"Words that sound the same but mean different things, like sea and see.",
 jl:"Some words sound exactly the same but are spelled differently and mean different things. Sea is where fish swim, and see is what you do with your eyes. They are called homophones.",
 jx:"Our teacher wrote pair and pear on the board to show us homophones."},
{id:"g_acronym",cat:"gen",t:"Acronym",
 s:"A word formed from the first letters of other words and spoken as a word.",
 l:"An acronym is made from the initial letters of a phrase and pronounced as a single word, like NATO or scuba. When the letters are said one by one, like BBC or USA, the result is usually called an initialism, though many people call both acronyms. Some acronyms become so common that people forget they were ever abbreviations and write them in lower case. When writing for listeners, it helps to explain an acronym the first time you use it.",
 x:"Radar is an acronym for radio detection and ranging.",
 f:"Laser started as an acronym for light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation.",
 js:"A short word made from the first letters of other words.",
 jl:"Sometimes long names are shortened by taking the first letter of each word. If you can say it like a word, it's an acronym. Scuba, for diving, is one of them!",
 jx:"We made an acronym for our club: CATS, for Cool Animals Team School."},
{id:"g_alliteration",cat:"gen",t:"Alliteration",
 s:"The repetition of the same sound at the beginning of several nearby words.",
 l:"Alliteration repeats a starting consonant sound in words close together, as in big bad bear or wild and windy weather. It makes phrases rhythmic and memorable, so it is common in poetry, tongue twisters, headlines, brand names and advertising slogans. What counts is the sound, not the letter, so city and soup alliterate, while cat and city do not. Used too much, it can sound silly, which is part of the fun of tongue twisters.",
 x:"Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers is a famous example of alliteration.",
 f:"Many cartoon and comic characters have alliterative names, which makes them easy to remember.",
 js:"When several words close together start with the same sound.",
 jl:"Say this fast: silly snakes slide slowly. All the words start with the same sound! That is called alliteration. Tongue twisters use it to make your tongue trip up.",
 jx:"For her poem, Amy wrote: busy bees buzz by the blue bush."},
{id:"g_rhetorical_question",cat:"gen",t:"Rhetorical question",
 s:"A question asked for effect, not because the speaker expects an answer.",
 l:"A rhetorical question is used to make a point, show feeling or make listeners think, rather than to get information. Who wouldn't want a day off? really means everyone would. Speakers and writers use them to involve the audience, emphasise an idea or express surprise or frustration. Because the expected answer is obvious, replying to one can sound funny or cheeky. In everyday life, What time do you call this? is usually a rhetorical question, not a request for the time.",
 x:"Is the sky blue? he replied, meaning yes, obviously.",
 f:"Rhetoric, the art of persuasive speaking, was taught as a main school subject in ancient Greece and Rome.",
 js:"A question you ask without wanting an answer.",
 jl:"Sometimes people ask a question but don't really want an answer. When Mum says, how many times have I told you?, she doesn't want a number! She's making a point. That's a rhetorical question.",
 jx:"Who wants homework at the weekend? our teacher joked – a rhetorical question!"},
{id:"g_en_cuppa",cat:"gen",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Cuppa",
 s:"An informal British word for a cup of tea.",
 l:"Cuppa is a friendly, casual way to say a cup of, almost always meaning tea. Fancy a cuppa? is a common British offer of tea and often of a chat, comfort or a break as well. Offering a cuppa is a typical reaction to bad news, a visitor arriving or the end of a hard task. The phrase to put the kettle on carries a similar meaning. Cuppa is informal, so it suits friends and colleagues rather than formal writing.",
 x:"You look exhausted – sit down and I'll make you a cuppa.",
 f:"Cuppa simply shows how cup of sounds in quick, relaxed speech, where of is reduced to a short a.",
 js:"A friendly British word for a cup of tea.",
 jl:"In Britain, people often say, fancy a cuppa? It means, would you like a cup of tea? Grown-ups often make a cuppa when a visitor comes or when someone needs cheering up.",
 jx:"When Grandma visited, Dad asked her: fancy a cuppa?"},
/* ---------- PSYCHOLOGY (+6) ---------- */
{id:"p_self_compassion",cat:"psy",t:"Self-compassion",
 s:"Treating yourself with the same kindness and understanding you would offer a good friend.",
 l:"Self-compassion means responding to your own mistakes and difficult moments with warmth instead of harsh criticism. The psychologist Kristin Neff describes three parts: being kind to yourself, remembering that struggling is part of being human, and noticing painful feelings without being swallowed by them. It is not the same as self-pity or letting yourself off the hook; people who are kinder to themselves often find it easier to learn from mistakes and try again.",
 x:"Instead of calling herself stupid, she practised self-compassion and said: everyone makes mistakes.",
 f:"A simple self-compassion exercise is to ask: what would I say to a friend in this situation? and then say it to yourself.",
 js:"Being kind to yourself, like you would be to a good friend.",
 jl:"When your friend makes a mistake, you might say: don't worry, you'll do better next time. Self-compassion means talking to yourself in the same kind way when you make a mistake.",
 jx:"When Mia got a spelling wrong, she told herself: it's okay, I'm still learning."},
{id:"p_habit",cat:"psy",t:"Habit",
 s:"A behaviour repeated so often in the same situation that it happens almost automatically.",
 l:"Habits form when we repeat an action in a similar context until it needs little thought, such as brushing teeth after breakfast or checking the phone when we sit down. Psychologists often describe a loop: a cue, the routine, and a reward that makes us likely to repeat it. Habits save mental effort, which is useful, but they also make unhelpful patterns hard to change. Building a new habit usually works best by linking it to an existing cue and starting very small.",
 x:"Putting her running shoes by the door helped her build the habit of a morning walk.",
 f:"The word habit comes from the Latin habitus, a condition or way of being; the same root gives the habit worn by monks and nuns.",
 js:"Something you do so often that you do it without thinking.",
 jl:"Do you brush your teeth without even thinking about it? That's a habit. If you do the same thing again and again at the same time, your brain learns it, and then it becomes easy.",
 jx:"Packing my school bag every evening has become a habit."},
{id:"p_introversion",cat:"psy",t:"Introversion and extraversion",
 s:"Personality traits describing whether a person tends to recharge in quiet, calm settings or in lively social ones.",
 l:"Introversion and extraversion describe a range, not two boxes. More introverted people often prefer smaller groups and quiet time and may feel drained after much social activity; more extraverted people often gain energy from company and busy surroundings. Many people are somewhere in between, sometimes called ambiverts. Introversion is not the same as shyness, which is fear of social judgement. Neither trait is better; each brings different strengths.",
 x:"As an introvert, he enjoys parties but needs a quiet evening afterwards.",
 f:"The Swiss psychiatrist Carl Gustav Jung popularised the terms introversion and extraversion in his book Psychological Types, published in nineteen twenty-one.",
 js:"Some people get energy from being with others, some from quiet time.",
 jl:"Some people love big parties and feel full of energy with lots of friends. Others like playing with one friend or being quiet with a book. Both are completely fine! People are just different.",
 jx:"After the noisy birthday party, Ella liked to read quietly in her room."},
{id:"p_sunk_cost",cat:"psy",t:"Sunk cost fallacy",
 s:"Continuing with something because of what you have already invested, even when stopping would be better.",
 l:"A sunk cost is time, money or effort that has already been spent and cannot be recovered. The sunk cost fallacy happens when this past investment, rather than future benefits, drives our decisions, such as sitting through a terrible film because the ticket was paid for. Good decisions look at what will happen from now on. People fall into this trap partly because stopping feels like admitting a loss. Noticing the pattern can make it easier to change course.",
 x:"I'd already paid for the course, but staying in it when I hated it was the sunk cost fallacy.",
 f:"It is sometimes called the Concorde fallacy, after the supersonic airliner whose development continued despite growing doubts that it would ever pay for itself.",
 js:"Keeping on with something just because you already started, even when it isn't good any more.",
 jl:"Imagine you start a really boring game and you've played for an hour. You keep going only because you already spent an hour on it, even though you'd be happier stopping. That's the sunk cost fallacy.",
 jx:"I stopped reading the boring book halfway – no sunk cost fallacy for me!"},
{id:"p_hyperfocus",cat:"psy",t:"Hyperfocus",
 s:"A state of intense, long-lasting concentration on one activity, often while losing track of time and surroundings.",
 l:"Hyperfocus means becoming so absorbed in something interesting that everything else fades away, sometimes for hours. Many people with ADHD and many autistic people describe it, which shows that ADHD is not simply a lack of attention but difficulty steering it. Hyperfocus can be a real strength for creative work, learning and problem-solving. The downside is forgetting meals, breaks or appointments, so some people use alarms or reminders to come up for air.",
 x:"In hyperfocus, he coded until three in the morning without noticing the time.",
 f:"Hyperfocus resembles the flow state that psychologists describe, but it is often harder to start and stop at will.",
 js:"Being so busy with something you love that you forget everything else.",
 jl:"Sometimes you are so into building, drawing or reading that you don't hear anyone calling you. Some people's brains do this a lot. It's called hyperfocus. It can help you do amazing things, but timers help remind you to eat and rest.",
 jx:"Max was in hyperfocus with his Lego and didn't hear the dinner bell."},
{id:"p_en_blue_monday",cat:"psy",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Blue Monday",
 s:"A date in January promoted in the UK as the most depressing day of the year, a claim with no scientific basis.",
 l:"Blue Monday, usually the third Monday in January, became famous in Britain after a publicity campaign by a travel company in two thousand and five, which presented a formula combining weather, debt and broken New Year's resolutions. Scientists and mental health charities point out that the formula is not real science and that low mood cannot be pinned to one date. Some charities now use the day to encourage people to talk about mental health and to challenge the idea of a single saddest day.",
 x:"The news called it Blue Monday, but the psychologist said the idea was a marketing myth.",
 f:"The idea was created as part of an advertising campaign meant to encourage people to book holidays, not as the result of a scientific study.",
 js:"A day in January that people call the saddest day, though it's really just a made-up idea.",
 jl:"Some people say a Monday in January is the saddest day of the year. But that idea was made up to sell holidays. Scientists say feelings don't work by the calendar. Any day can be good or hard.",
 jx:"Our teacher said Blue Monday is a myth, and we made it a kindness day instead."},
/* ---------- BUSINESS (+6) ---------- */
{id:"b_target_audience",cat:"biz",t:"Target audience",
 s:"The particular group of people a product, service or message is mainly aimed at.",
 l:"A target audience, or target market, is defined by features such as age, interests, needs, location or income. Knowing it helps a business choose what to offer, how to price it, where to advertise and what tone to use. A skateboard shop and a retirement planner speak to very different people. Targeting does not mean ignoring everyone else; it means focusing limited time and money where they are most likely to work. Businesses often describe their typical customer as a persona.",
 x:"Our target audience is busy parents, so the app must be quick to use.",
 f:"Marketers often create personas, short imaginary profiles with a name, age and habits, to keep the target audience in mind.",
 js:"The people something is mainly made for.",
 jl:"When companies make something, they think about who it's for. A picture book is for young children, a gardening magazine for people who like plants. Those people are the target audience.",
 jx:"Our school play's target audience was parents and grandparents."},
{id:"b_net_gross",cat:"biz",t:"Net and gross",
 s:"Gross is an amount before deductions; net is what remains after them.",
 l:"Gross means the total amount before anything is taken off, while net is what is left afterwards. Gross pay is a salary before income tax and National Insurance; net pay, or take-home pay, is what actually reaches the bank account. In business, gross profit is sales minus the direct cost of goods, while net profit also subtracts all other costs and taxes. Mixing up the two words can lead to very different expectations.",
 x:"The job offered thirty thousand pounds gross, which is noticeably less net.",
 f:"Both words came into English through trade with Italy and France: net comes from a word meaning clean, and gross from a word meaning big or coarse.",
 js:"Gross is the whole amount; net is what is left after taking some away.",
 jl:"Imagine you earn ten pounds for washing cars, but you spend two pounds on soap. Ten pounds is the gross amount. The eight pounds left over is the net amount.",
 jx:"Our lemonade stand made twenty pounds gross and fifteen net after buying lemons."},
{id:"b_vat",cat:"biz",c:"United Kingdom",t:"VAT (Value Added Tax)",
 s:"A tax added to the price of most goods and services, collected by businesses for the government.",
 l:"VAT is charged at each step of the supply chain, but businesses can usually reclaim the VAT they paid on their own purchases, so in the end the tax is mainly paid by the final consumer. In the UK, businesses must register for VAT once their taxable turnover passes a threshold set by the government. The standard rate is twenty percent; some goods have a reduced rate, and others, such as most food and children's clothes, are zero-rated. Prices for consumers are usually shown including VAT.",
 x:"The invoice shows the price before VAT and the total including VAT.",
 f:"VAT was introduced in the UK in nineteen seventy-three, the year the country joined the European Economic Community.",
 js:"A tax that is added to the price of many things we buy.",
 jl:"When you buy a toy, part of the price goes to the government as a tax called VAT. The shop collects it and passes it on. The government uses tax money for things like schools, roads and hospitals.",
 jx:"Dad explained that part of the price of my new trainers was VAT."},
{id:"b_merger",cat:"biz",t:"Merger and acquisition",
 s:"A merger joins two companies into one; an acquisition is when one company buys another.",
 l:"In a merger, two companies agree to combine into a single business, often of similar size. In an acquisition, or takeover, one company buys control of another, which may keep its name or be absorbed. Companies do this to grow, enter new markets, gain technology or cut costs. Large deals are checked by competition authorities, which can block them if they would harm customers by reducing competition. Together they are often shortened to M and A.",
 x:"After the merger, the two banks closed some branches that stood on the same street.",
 f:"A takeover that the target company's management does not want is called a hostile takeover.",
 js:"When two companies join together, or one buys the other.",
 jl:"Sometimes two companies decide to become one big company. That's a merger. Other times a big company buys a smaller one. That's an acquisition. It's a bit like two teams joining into one team.",
 jx:"Our two school clubs had a merger and became one big art and science club."},
{id:"b_bootstrapping",cat:"biz",t:"Bootstrapping",
 s:"Starting and growing a business with your own money and income from sales, without outside investors.",
 l:"A bootstrapped business is funded by the founders' savings and the money customers pay, rather than by investors or large loans. This keeps the founders fully in control and forces careful spending, but growth can be slower and personal financial risk higher. Many small businesses and some well-known companies began this way. Bootstrapping is the opposite of raising venture capital, where investors give money in exchange for a share of the company.",
 x:"They bootstrapped the bakery for two years before taking a bank loan.",
 f:"The phrase comes from pulling yourself up by your bootstraps, which originally described something impossible; the computer word booting comes from the same image.",
 js:"Starting a business with your own money, without help from investors.",
 jl:"Bootstrapping means starting a small business by yourself, with your own savings, and using the money you earn to make it bigger. It's like starting a lemonade stand with your pocket money and buying more lemons with what you sell.",
 jx:"Jake bootstrapped his bracelet business with his birthday money."},
{id:"b_en_hmrc",cat:"biz",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"HMRC",
 s:"His Majesty's Revenue and Customs, the UK government department that collects taxes.",
 l:"HMRC collects taxes such as income tax, National Insurance, VAT and corporation tax, and runs customs at the border. It also pays some benefits and runs the system that gives people their tax code. Self-employed people and others with untaxed income usually send a Self Assessment tax return to HMRC each year; the online deadline is the thirty-first of January. HMRC never asks for bank details by text message, so such messages are often scams.",
 x:"As a freelancer, she sends her tax return to HMRC every January.",
 f:"HMRC was created in two thousand and five by merging the Inland Revenue with HM Customs and Excise.",
 js:"The part of the UK government that collects taxes.",
 jl:"HMRC is the office that collects tax money for the country. People and businesses pay part of what they earn, and HMRC collects it. That money helps pay for schools, hospitals and roads.",
 jx:"Mum said she had to fill in a form for HMRC about her new business."}
]);
